text:
Bayard Rustin was a chief organizer of the 1963 March on Washington and thought reparations, and even separate African-American studies departments, were a bad idea. Many of his beliefs would
be antithetical to
today’s social justice advocates. In the video above, Coleman Hughes argues that by cherry-picking our heroes, and focusing on small parts of their legacy, we are merely paying lip service to their mission.
Coleman Hughes (
@coldxman
) is a columnist for the online magazine Quillette and an undergraduate student at Columbia University.
